Four people died and at least 10 went missing after an avalanche struck a climbing camp in Nepal, just weeks after deadly flash floods left nearly 1,400 dead in the Himalayan nation.

The avalanche hit the base camp of Himlung Himal, a 7,126m peak in the Nar Phu area of Manang district, close to the border with Tibet, China, on Sunday morning.

It buried a tent in which nine climbers and 13 support staff were sheltering.

Deputy police superintendent Shailendra Thapa said on Sunday that 12 people went missing after the avalanche and two were later found dead on the peak, leaving 10, all Nepalese nationals, unaccounted for.

