The Nepalese guides and support staff had been sent ahead of foreign climbers to prepare the camp and fix ropes, according to local media reports. Poor weather hampered the rescue, with helicopters and rescue teams initially unable to reach the site.

A team of experienced mountaineers was then flown in from the capital Kathmandu.

Farmland lies covered in mud after flash floods in Galchi in Nepal ( AP )

Persistent rainfall and snow in mountainous areas in recent days have created treacherous conditions for Nepal’s climbing tourism industry.

A few days ago, climbers were forced to leave Mount Manaslu, the world's eighth highest peak, because of the rising threat of avalanches.

The avalanche on Himlung Himal came weeks after a glacial collapse in August sent floods and debris down the Trishuli River valley in the country, killing at least 1,400 people and leaving some 6,000 still missing.

Extreme weather has affected many of Asia in the past week.

Nepal’s disaster management agency said 21 other people had been killed and five were missing in different parts of the country between Thursday and Sunday.

More than 50 people have been killed in India's northern state of Uttar Pradesh, according to officials, and a flood emergency has been declared in Bangkok, Thailand. Earlier, Typhoon Dujuan, the latest in a series of Pacific storms made more intense by the weather phenomenon "super El Niño", struck Japan and triggered landslides that killed four people.
